WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.186 --> 00:00:02.340
(tense tonal music)
(birds calling)

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:03.770
- [Narrator] There are few industries

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.880
that have been harder hit by the pandemic

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.340
than the cruise industry.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:08.920
- Normally, these ships would be packed

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:12.350
with thousands of
passengers and crew members.

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:14.440
But this particular ship is almost empty.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.890
It's just a skeleton crew maintaining it.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.990
- It's very, very difficult to
go this long with a business

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:21.560
and not have any income.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.640
- [Narrator] On March 13, 2020,

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.970
the CDC issued a no sail order
for ships in American waters,

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:32.003
essentially placing the entire
cruise industry on pause.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.480
And that pause has been expensive.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.490
According to a recent analysis,

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.660
last year's suspension
resulted in an estimated loss

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:45.547
of over $32 billion in economic
activity and 254,000 jobs.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.670
But the impact of those
numbers isn't just confined

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.430
to the cruise lines and their crews.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.630
The cruise industry's
crisis is also being felt

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:59.373
in cruise-dependent
businesses, like ports.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.270
Port Canaveral is located
on Florida's Atlantic coast,

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:06.233
50 miles outside of Orlando.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.740
This is Port Canaveral's
brand new cruise terminal.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.280
Last June, after four
years of construction,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.190
the $155 million terminal was finished

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.363
just in time for summer sailing season.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.430
But to date, no passenger has
ever set foot in Terminal 3.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.740
In fact, it's now been over
a year since any passengers

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:31.030
have set sail on a cruise
ship in American waters

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:32.430
leaving many wondering,

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.350
when the ships finally be
able to set sail again?

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:38.670
- I'm here at Port Canaveral's
brand new Terminal 3,

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.930
which in normal times would
welcome thousands of passengers

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.330
for Carnival Cruise's new ship.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:48.150
But because of the pandemic
there's absolutely no one here.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:49.550
It's a vacant parking lot,

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.370
a huge, brand new vacant building.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:55.460
Like the rest of the cruise
industry in the United States,

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:56.820
Port Canaveral is awaiting

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.130
further instructions from Washington

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.070
as to how to safely open
during the pandemic.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.810
But despite lobbying from
all of the cruise lines

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:08.010
and everyone else connected to
the industry, like the ports,

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.450
Washington has been slow to issue guidance

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:13.920
as to how to restart
cruises in the United States

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.690
and hasn't really given much financial aid

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:18.523
to places like Port Canaveral.

00:02:19.404 --> 00:02:21.980
- This will deliver urgently needed relief

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.810
to our nation's families,
workers and businesses.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.340
- [Narrator] Last year,
when airlines, hotels,

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.050
and other travel-related industries

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.950
received government assistance,

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.980
many cruise-related
businesses were left out.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:37.310
- We've been down,
basically, for a full year,

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:40.210
as of this week and there's no end

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:42.540
to the downturn at this point.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:43.620
- [Narrator] Captain John Murray

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.290
is the CEO of Port Canaveral,
which before the pandemic

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:50.260
was the second busiest
cruise port in the world.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.710
- You know, I'd not like to
even refer to it as a bailout,

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:56.090
it's to sustain our business operations,

00:02:56.090 --> 00:03:01.090
sustain the strategic
aspect of the seaport.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:04.600
- [Narrator] Many US ports
are a mix of cruise and cargo,

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:05.960
but at Port Canaveral,

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:09.320
the cruise side makes up
about 80% of its revenue.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.400
To make ends meet, the port
has been surviving on cash

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.210
it had set aside for construction.

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:17.260
- We went from making
reasonable amount of money

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.350
down to nothing overnight.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:20.750
To losing money overnight.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.530
It's been quite a shock to our system.

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.650
We'll be fine for the
balance of this year,

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.230
but at some point the
cash we're burning now

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:28.313
will eventually run out.

00:03:28.313 --> 00:03:30.110
(dramatic tonal music)

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.340
- [Narrator] According to Murray,

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.937
every year the port generates

00:03:32.937 --> 00:03:37.777
$2.6 billion in economic
impact, 24,000 jobs,

00:03:37.777 --> 00:03:42.777
$900 million in wages, and
$74 million in tax revenue.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.800
- This has been a tremendous impact,

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.320
had a tremendous impact on our community,

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:50.150
everyone that supports
the cruise industry,

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:52.490
a lot of jobs have been sidelined.

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:53.500
All the longshoremen,

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.170
the folks that load and
discharge the ships,

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.150
they're not doing anything right now.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.980
And in the prior stimulus
packages that have come out,

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.890
the airlines have got
a vehicle to get money,

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:07.600
the airports, rapid transit, railroads,

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:10.880
everybody's had a vehicle
and seaports never did.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.310
So no seaports in the United States

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:16.405
have had any federal
relief up to this point.

00:04:16.405 --> 00:04:21.405
(tense tonal music)
(birds calling)

